WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:04.730
The first data type we will cover is the string
data type.

00:00:04.730 --> 00:00:09.460
Strings of text go inside quotation marks
and are very versatile in how they are used

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.110
in the context of a program.

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:19.340
In this first code example, a file called
111-str, we see two variables with strings:

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.160
one containing a first name and one containing
a last name.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:29.080
Before getting more into strings, let’s
go through a basic programming tenet: variables.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:35.070
A variable is a container that stores a value,
like a string of text, a number, or other

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:36.090
data types.

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:44.490
I have two such variables on lines 1 and 2,
first_name and last_name.

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:50.410
Variable names can contain letters, numbers,
and underscores but not symbols.

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.020
They cannot contain spaces.

00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:58.520
If you have any experience with C# or Java
or JavaScript, you may have seen variables

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:05.019
using camel case, which makes the first word
lowercase, and all subsequent words start

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:06.930
with a capital letter.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.940
You can use camel casing in Python, but the
standard naming convention in Python is to

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.990
use all lowercase characters with underscores
replacing spaces.

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:27.580
I want to output this as a full name, so on
line 3, I will type print(last_name +”,

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:42.710
“ + first_name) with a space before the
comma.

00:01:42.710 --> 00:01:45.460
A print statement creates output.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:51.390
Within that print statement, any literal text,
like the comma and space we see here, must

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.220
be inside quotation marks.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.511
Let’s run this code in a terminal to ensure
it works.

00:01:57.511 --> 00:02:06.600
I will select the Run Python File icon, and
below, on the Terminal tab, we will see the

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.580
results: my last name, comma, and first name.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:16.140
Though not on the exam, one question when
learning a programming language is: do we

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:21.000
use single quotes around text strings or double
quotes?

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.080
Double quotes work.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.860
But what if I change the quotes inside the
print statement to single quotes?

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:33.120
I will rerun this code, and it works.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:37.720
This output speaks to Python's versatility,
where we can use single or double quotes but

00:02:37.720 --> 00:02:43.250
not curly quotes, as is often the case with
quotes pasted from Word, so be aware of that

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:44.250
possible issue.

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:49.480
You may be wondering when the quotes used
matter, then.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.360
Let me show you an example of when it does
matter.

00:02:52.360 --> 00:03:00.360
I will skip a line and add this print statement
to line 5: print (‘Jason’s turn’) and

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:08.020
then attempt to run this code.

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.640
Notice that we get an error message.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.090
Why?

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:19.640
Because Python thinks we have an opening quote
here with no closing quote, which will generate

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.110
an error message.

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:31.900
But, if I change the quotes here to double
quotes, this runs again because, now, the

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:35.900
single quote is ignoring these double quotes.

